	"identifying_description": "Leaf of vellum and paper with opaque watercolor and gilding, with scene of couple before the altar of Hymen with gods and goddesses arriving by land, sea, and air. At left, Cupid puts a torch to the weapons of Mars. On reverse, two women and a man sit on an island with a ruined column. Sticks and guards of carved and incised mother-of-pearl with pastoral figures, musical putti, and rocaille ornament. Glass jewel and silvered copper alloy rivet.",
	"web_text": "Amorous scenes became widespread in the eighteenth-century visual arts, including fan designs, coinciding with increased societal interest in expressions of romantic love. Fans with illustrations of wedding themes were often given to brides-to-be as tokens of affection from their fiancés, or by brides to their attendants. In this iteration of the wedding theme, Hymen, the god of marriage, kindles the marital fire at his altar as the bride and groom walk toward each other to be wed.",
	"didactic_label": "Amorous scenes became widespread in the eighteenth-century visual arts, including fan designs, coinciding with increased societal interest in expressions of romantic love. Fans with illustrations of wedding themes were often given to brides-to-be as tokens of affection from their fiancés, or by brides to their attendants. In this iteration of the wedding theme, Hymen, the god of marriage, kindles the marital fire at his altar as the bride and groom walk toward each other to be wed.",
	"exhibition_history_text": "Fans of the Eighteenth Century, Textiles Education Gallery, de Young Museum, 3/31/18 - 11/2/18\nGallery 9A, Legion of Honor, 4/04 - 10/04\nFans in Fashion, Legion of Honor, 5/23/81 - 8/30/81",
